\title{peabiru: multimodal routing on OpenStreetMap and GTFS networks}
\description{
peabiru builds a network from an 'OpenStreetMap' extract, 'GTFS' feeds
and an optional elevation raster with \code{\link[=build_network]{build_network()}}, and computes,
by walking, cycling, driving and public transport:
travel-time matrices (\code{\link[=travel_time_matrix]{travel_time_matrix()}},
\code{\link[=expanded_travel_time_matrix]{expanded_travel_time_matrix()}}, \code{\link[=arrival_travel_time_matrix]{arrival_travel_time_matrix()}}),
accessibility (\code{\link[=accessibility]{accessibility()}}), isochrones (\code{\link[=isochrone]{isochrone()}}) and
detailed itineraries (\code{\link[=detailed_itineraries]{detailed_itineraries()}}), with or without fares
(\code{\link[=setup_fare_structure]{setup_fare_structure()}}, \code{\link[=read_fare_structure]{read_fare_structure()}},
\code{\link[=write_fare_structure]{write_fare_structure()}}, \code{\link[=pareto_frontier]{pareto_frontier()}}) and scenarios of car
speeds and traffic stress. It inspects a network with \code{\link[=find_snap]{find_snap()}},
\code{\link[=street_network_bbox]{street_network_bbox()}}, \code{\link[=street_network_to_sf]{street_network_to_sf()}},
\code{\link[=transit_network_to_sf]{transit_network_to_sf()}}, \code{\link[=check_transit_availability]{check_transit_availability()}} and
\code{\link[=get_gtfs_errors]{get_gtfs_errors()}}. The routing engine is written in 'Rust' and runs
inside the R session; no 'Java' is needed. What the package does not
support is an error that says so, never silently ignored.
}
\details{
The routing engine is a 'Rust' port of Conveyal's 'R5' routing engine, and
the functions are modelled on those of the 'r5r' package (Ipea), from
whose 'Java' code the fare calculator and parts of the trip planner are
translated; see the references, and the article "Compared with r5r" on
the package website. peabiru is an independent project, not affiliated
with or endorsed by Conveyal, Ipea or the r5r team.
}
\section{Class of the results}{

The tables the functions return are tibbles by default. The option
\code{peabiru.output}, read at each call, chooses another class:
\itemize{
\item \code{options(peabiru.output = "tibble")} (the default; 'tibble' is the
package's one dependency): tibbles, and \code{sf} tibbles (class
\code{c("sf", "tbl_df", "tbl", "data.frame")}) for the results with a
geometry, as \code{sf::st_as_sf()} makes of a tibble;
\item \code{options(peabiru.output = "data.frame")}: base \code{data.frame}s, and plain
\code{sf} data frames;
\item \code{options(peabiru.output = "data.table")} (needs 'data.table'):
\code{data.table}s: \code{sf} \code{data.table}s for isochrone polygons,
itineraries, the street edges and the transit routes, plain \code{sf} data
frames for isochrone lines, street vertices and transit stops, and an
index on the column \code{found} of \code{\link[=find_snap]{find_snap()}}. A good choice for large
matrices handled with 'data.table'.
}

Any other value is an error. The option applies to every table: the
matrices, accessibility, Pareto frontiers, \code{\link[=find_snap]{find_snap()}},
\code{\link[=check_transit_availability]{check_transit_availability()}}, \code{\link[=get_gtfs_errors]{get_gtfs_errors()}}, the tables of a fare
structure, the tables of \code{\link[=trip_options]{trip_options()}} and the \code{sf} results (whose
geometries are always in EPSG:4326). The rows, the columns, their order
and types, and the row names are the same whatever the class.
}

\section{Sample data}{

\code{system.file("extdata/spo", package = "peabiru")} holds a small network
for examples and tests, cut from the Sao Paulo sample data of the r5r
project: \code{spo_osm.pbf}, the streets around Avenida Paulista (data
(c) OpenStreetMap contributors, available under the Open Database
License, \url{https://www.openstreetmap.org/copyright}); \code{spo_gtfs.zip}, the
SPTrans frequency-based 'GTFS' feed of that sample, without
\code{shapes.txt}; and \code{spo_points.csv}, 27 points of its hexagonal grid with
their population and opportunity counts.
}

